{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` is **799 LOC** against the locked\n800-LOC hard cap (`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), so the next agent to touch it — even to add a\ncomment — fails the CI module-size gate. Gerrit 1916 failed exactly that way on a sibling file.\n\n## Approach\nSplit along the seam the module's own **import graph and call graph already draw**: the file holds\ntwo disjoint function clusters that share no module-level import except `logging`.\n\n**Cluster A — event-file materialisation (MOVES OUT).** `_RECONCILER_MARKER_APPLIER` plus the four\n`_inbound_create_*` phase helpers (`apply_inbound_records.py:153-339`) and the four\n`_inbound_update_write_edit_event` / `_write_status_event` / `_apply_labels` / `_apply_comments`\nhelpers (`:347-545`). Every one of them terminates in `inbound_translate._write_event_file`, and\nthey are the ONLY consumers of this module's entire module-level import block\n(`projects_store`, `batch_dispatch._call_with_retry`, `_backend.TicketTransport`, `sys`, and all\n13 names imported from `rebar_reconciler.inbound_translate` at `:31-44`).\n\n**Cluster B — rebar-facade mutation (STAYS).** `_identity_provider_for` /\n`_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity` (`:54-145`) and the closed link sub-graph\n`_inbound_update_apply_links` → `_open_impossible_link_store` / `_open_peer_confirmation_store` /\n`_skip_impossible_link` / `_note_impossible_link` / `_inbound_unlink_one` (`:548-799`). These write\nthrough the public `rebar` facade (`rebar.ensure_identity_for`, `rebar.link`, `rebar.unlink`) and\nthe two sidecar stores, are all best-effort/fail-open with `logger` reporting, and write NO event\nfiles. Every dependency they have is a function-local import already, so they need none of the\nmodule-level block.\n\nCluster A moves verbatim to a new sibling `apply_inbound_events.py`. Cluster B keeps the existing\nfile (and therefore its `logger` and its module identity), because two existing tests are bound to\nthat module OBJECT and would silently stop testing anything if the cluster moved:\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_removal_confirmation_a4bd.py:188`\nmonkeypatches `_open_peer_confirmation_store` on it, and\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_link_removal_2b16.py:643` captures logs on\n`apply_records.logger.name`. Keeping cluster B in place preserves both without editing them.\n\nThe two cluster-A helpers that call `_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity` (`:214`, `:388`) import it\nfunction-locally from `apply_inbound_records` — the idiom this module already uses for every\n`rebar`/sibling dependency, and it preserves today's call-time (late) binding exactly, so an\nexisting monkeypatch of that name still takes effect. That direction keeps the import graph\nacyclic: `apply_inbound_records` imports `apply_inbound_events` at module level for one documented\nback-compat re-export (`_inbound_update_write_edit_event`, driven as\n`air._inbound_update_write_edit_event` by\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_inbound_update_assignee_mint_5200.py:241,285`); the events module\nnever imports the records module at module level.\n\nWhy this approach: it is the only seam that (a) follows existing call-graph clusters rather than a\nline count, (b) leaves both halves in the 200-500 LOC target band with 350+ lines of headroom each,\nand (c) requires zero changes to any behavioural assertion.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_events.py` — NEW; receives cluster A verbatim.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` — keeps cluster B; drops the now-unused\n  module-level imports; one documented re-export.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound.py` — repoint its phase-helper imports (`:34-47`)\n  at the new module; `_inbound_update_apply_links` still comes from `apply_inbound_records`.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_inbound_apply_seam_split.py` — NEW seam regression test.\n- Path-keyed anchors that name the OLD location of moved lines and must be retargeted in this same\n  change (each is a location anchor; no assertion is weakened):\n  - `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_neutrality.py:62` — the single-`\"jira\"`-literal census\n    is keyed to `apply_inbound_records.py`, but the literal lives in\n    `validate_creation_channel(\"jira\")` at `:222`, inside cluster A.\n  - `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_live_oracles_discriminate_5200.py:217` — its `writers`\n    tuple is keyed on file name (its docstring says it \"must be retargeted whenever one moves\");\n    the `f\"rebar-id:{local_id}\"` literal it asserts is at `:292`, inside cluster A.\n  - `tests/scripts/test_reconciler_core_seam_clean_heldout.py:54` — the config-ownership\n    `_OWNED_FILES` registry; the new module is added so the split cannot shrink that gate's\n    coverage. Its `_MARKER_BUDGET` / `_TOTAL_MARKER_CAP` are untouched: the new file carries zero\n    `# read-via:` markers.\n- `docs/code-navigation.md:61,80` and `docs/event-schema.md:261` — citations of moved symbols;\n  retargeted while keeping the old file name visible so\n  `tests/unit/test_agents_serena_untyped_receiver.py:62` still finds it. ADRs are historical records\n  and are left alone.\n\nAssume no other consumer exists: verified with Serena `find_referencing_symbols` plus a plain `grep`\nfor every moved symbol name AS A STRING across `src`, `tests`, `scripts`, `docs`.\n\n## Testing\nNew `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_inbound_apply_seam_split.py`, written RED before the\nmove (both assertions fail against today's single file):\n- **Happy path / seam pinned:** `apply_inbound_records.py` contains no `_write_event_file(` call and\n  `apply_inbound_events.py` contains no `rebar.link(` / `rebar.unlink(` / `ensure_identity_for(`\n  call — the concern boundary cannot silently re-merge.\n- **Edge / headroom:** both modules are >= 100 LOC and <= the near-cap band computed from the\n  single-sourced limit in `.github/module-size-limit.txt` (`LIMIT - LIMIT // 10` = 720), so neither\n  half lands back in the warning list. Reuses `tests/module_size_support` rather than restating the\n  limit.\n- **Import-surface preservation:** every one of the nine phase helpers plus\n  `_RECONCILER_MARKER_APPLIER` still resolves through `rebar_reconciler.apply_inbound`, so the\n  orchestrator's contract is unchanged.\nRegression coverage is the existing suite, run unmodified:\n`pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler tests/scripts -m \"not integration and not external\"`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `wc -l src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` is <= 720 (real headroom,\n      not one or two lines) and `apply_inbound_events.py` is between 100 and 720 — checked by the new\n      seam test and by `wc -l`.\n- [ ] `.github/module-size-limit.txt` is byte-identical to `origin/main` — checked with\n      `git diff origin/main -- .github/module-size-limit.txt` returning empty.\n- [ ] The new seam test fails on the pre-split tree and passes after — demonstrated by running it\n      before the move (RED) and after (GREEN).\n- [ ] No behavioural assertion is modified: the only test edits are the three path anchors named in\n      Scope, verified by reading `git diff -- tests/` and confirming each changed line is a file path\n      or a file-name tuple entry.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler tests/scripts -m \"not integration and not external\"` passes.\n- [ ] `make lint` (which includes `scripts/check_config_ownership.py` and the complexity ratchet\n      `--check`) and `make typecheck` pass, with the ratchet reporting `new=0 increased=0`.\n- [ ] Every moved symbol's references are updated — verified by BOTH Serena\n      `find_referencing_symbols` and a plain `grep` for each name as a string, recorded as a ticket\n      comment."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787181513501106001,"uuid":"8882e07e-d978-4e7f-acca-19ee66a43318"}